host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title Bounded for our own reasons, and inside a verified framework's numbers four times out of four
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, condense ITINERARY and refill Now, send each finished increment
think Lap index from git log --oneline | wc -l = 3423 at the open, so the council rota reads row 3423 mod 5 = 3 -- Water and Venus, care and flow: the shape you grew and the shape you are, the happy zone and the thin edge, growing a language. The Venus row named this lap before it started: the whole finding is about a shape Caravan grew for its own reasons turning out to be the shape it needed.
think Three rounds on the report card meter was enough on one instrument, and the prompt names the Microkernel Target double-seat as the Lindy priority. So I took the rung a lap two days ago had scoped and set down for Standfast: Microkit's protection-domain vocabulary as a third caravan rung beside refusals.rye and objects.rye.
think Chose the subject by measuring rather than by taste. Microkit's public header declares seven numbers bounding one protection domain, and TAME's first rule is bound everything -- so the rung writes itself if, and only if, Caravan's own bounds make a real accounting rather than a transcription. Checked before writing a line.
think They do. Four of the seven have a Caravan counterpart and every one sits inside: derivation.max_slots 32 inside a root cspace of 64, capabilities.max_dependents times max_caps_per_dependent 32 inside 128, channels.max_channels 16 inside 62, channels.max_name_len 48 inside 64. Every one of those four was chosen for its own written reason -- derivation.rye says a supervisor's cspace is a fixed allotment -- long before anyone here read this header.
think Verified the one mapping I was least sure of before claiming it. derivation.max_slots looked like a cspace counterpart and I read its own doc comment to check rather than assuming; it says exactly that. A wrong mapping here would have been a red wearing a finding's clothes.
think The second finding came out of the header's own shape: only four of the seven are independent. PD_ROOT_CAP_SIZE is the bits shifted, MICROKIT_MAX_CHANNEL_ID is the count less one, MICROKIT_MAX_IOPORT_ID is the count itself. A formally verified framework spells each governing number once and derives the rest, which is the law this tree booked the hard way as REDS %199 and %201.
think Made the invariant load-bearing rather than decorative by importing channels.rye, capabilities.rye and derivation.rye and comparing their actual constants. A copied number would have made the assert a sentence; an imported one makes widening a bound red on the lap that widens it rather than on the day of a port.
obs Built and self-tested GREEN on the first run, which is worth naming because it was not luck -- the numbers were all measured beforehand and the module only had to state what the measurement already said.
obs The symbol scan checks values as well as names, because here the numbers ARE the subject. refusals.rye once carried the right eleven names with two meanings swapped inside a parity check that looked only at names (REDS %107), and a names-only check would have been checking the least of this rung.
obs Made the scan resolve each derived value from the SOURCE the header names rather than reading its text as a number. That turned out to be the strongest reading: re-sourcing MICROKIT_MAX_CHANNEL_ID to a different constant drops derived_resolved from three to two, which a value-only check would read as agreement whenever the two values happened to coincide.
obs And moving MICROKIT_MAX_CHANNELS by one produces three mismatches rather than one, because the two derived numbers move with it. That is the derivation resolution proving itself live.
obs Then caught the vacuous plant, for the third time this session, and this one had already gone GREEN. The load-bearing test built the widened module in a /tmp pen, and rye resolves bare-name imports against the repository it runs from, so the build crashed, the binary never existed, and the assert on a non-zero exit passed on a missing file rather than on a refused invariant.
obs Rebuilt it honestly: a pen inside tools/.build with the whole import closure copied in, tally_copy.rye symlink included. The widened self-test now aborts with a named assertion failure at the invariant line, exit 134. And the control proves the WELCOME first -- the same pen with the bound left alone builds and passes -- so a broken pen can never read as a proof. That second half is the part I would have skipped an hour ago.
obs Three vacuous plants caught in one session, all of the same shape: a check that passes while testing nothing. Each was found by asking what would have to be true for the pass to mean something, rather than by rerunning it. Worth carrying: a green that arrives on the first try deserves the question more than a red does.
obs The caravan ladder roster caught the new module with no row, which is its job, and the row went in beside objects.
obs The hot roster reds readme_metrics and geode_libraries, which is the ordinary cost of adding a module and a witness in one round -- both pages count them and the pre-commit hook regenerates at commit. Regenerated by hand so the green measures the tree the commit ships: 1,685 witnesses to 1,686, 1,894 modules to 1,895, 39 library rows unchanged.
loom allowances declared=7 independent=4 derived=3 caravan_counterparts=4 all_inside=yes
